@begin(section)

@title(Contributing)

@i(Thank you so much for considering contribution to zick!)

A contribution can be large or small, code or non-code.  To make a
contribution, first log a GitHub issue.  Talk about what you want, and ask
for others' opinions.  The project tracks its work with
@link[uri="https://github.com/beads/beads"](Beads) issues in the repository
— run @c(bd ready) to see what work is available.

When you go to make the PR, please use the following checklist to test
whether or not it is likely to be accepted:

@begin(enum)
@item(@b(Is it based on the @c(main) branch?) Please make PRs to the
@c(main) branch.)
@item(@b(Do you have tests in your PR, and do they pass?) Tests are in two
places in zick: the @c(tests/) directory, where more or less normal unit
tests (using @link[uri="https://shinmera.github.io/parachute/"](Parachute))
reside; and the @c(tests/resources/scripts) directory, where scripty
integration tests reside (run against the built binary and the HTTPS fixture
server).  You must have at least a script test (and preferably one or more
unit tests) as a "spot-check" of your feature if the PR is to be merged.
The test need not be elaborate; a simple test is better than no tests.
Run the unit suites with @c(make test) and the black-box suite with
@c(make test-black-box); the black-box suite drives the built binary, so
run @c(./scripts/build) first.)
@item(@b(Did you add documentation around the feature in your PR?) Generally
this at least means adding something to the @c(Command Reference) section of
this manual (see the docs-writing instructions in the repository's
@c(.agents/skills/) directory), and regenerating the manual with
@c(make docs) to confirm the pages build and render.)
@item(@b(Did you add an entry to the Changelog?) This project keeps a
curated changelog in @c(CHANGELOG.md).)
@end(enum)

There are some exceptions to the above rules.  For example, if your patch is
less than two lines' difference from the previous version, your PR may be a
"typo" PR, which may qualify to get around some of the above rules.  Just
ask the team on your GitHub issue.

@end(section)
